nity.\n  - Melanie: Melanie congratulated Caroline on passing adoption agency interviews, viewing adoption as a loving way to build a family for needy kids, and encouraged Caroline to be herself.\n  - my own journey: Caroline shared details of her transgender journey, including struggles and development since coming out and starting transition three years ago, at a school event to inspire others.\n  - mentoring: Caroline finds the mentoring program rewarding, supporting amazing, resilient, and strong young folks; the experience is going great.\n  - journey: Caroline discussed her transgender journey, which started three years ago and involved volunteering at an LGBTQ+ youth center to support young people. Melanie praised her progress and authenticity.\n  - next time: Melanie asked Caroline what she hopes to do for the next time, following Caroline's volunteering experience.\n  - gender identity: Caroline mentioned that creating art, like painting and drawing, was important during her transition to understand and accept her gender identity.\n  - trans woman: Caroline shared a painting representing her path as a trans woman; colors symbolize smashing rigid thinking about the binary gender system and loving her authentic self.\n  - transgender community: Melanie asked Caroline what motivated her decision to transition and join the transgender community.\n  - LGBTQ advocacy stuff: Melanie asked Caroline about any upcoming exciting LGBTQ advocacy stuff after discussing Caroline's positive mentoring experiences.\n  - who I really am: Caroline found out who she really is with invaluable help from friends, family, and mentors during tough times, and now wants to pass that support to others.\n  - courage to transition: Caroline stated that realizing she could be herself without fear and having the courage to transition was the best part, calling it freeing to express herself authentically with support.\n  - binary gender system: Caroline made a painting representing her path